.\" Draft prepared from current source; see EDITORS-NOTES.md.
.\" Source: src/monitor.c, src/monitor-data.c, src/monitor-display.c
.TH "MONITOR" "1" "October 23, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
monitor \- listen to and display multicast receiver audio
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B monitor
.RI "[options] [data-destination ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
monitor receives one or more RTP audio groups, discovers stream formats from
metadata, decodes and resamples audio for playback, and displays active
sessions in a curses interface. By default active sessions are mixed and
positioned across stereo output. Voting can instead select a preferred
session. A destination may be a multicast address or a resolvable name. The
usual RTP data port is UDP 5004; the command/status port is UDP 5006. Use the
data destination for audio consumers and the status destination for
controllers. A multicast group may contain several streams distinguished by
SSRC.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-I destination, \-\-input destination"
Add an input data group. May be repeated; positional destinations are also
accepted.
.TP
.B "\-o source, \-\-source source"
Restrict reception to one network source.
.TP
.B "\-L, \-\-list\-audio"
List PortAudio devices and exit.
.TP
.B "\-R device, \-\-device device"
Select the PortAudio output device by name or numeric index. Default is the
PortAudio default output.
.TP
.B "\-P pathname, \-\-pipe pathname"
On Linux, write headerless native-endian signed 16-bit PCM to an existing
named pipe instead of ordinary playback. Rate and channel count follow the
playback settings. Opening the pipe can wait for a reader.
.TP
.B "\-r rate, \-\-samprate rate"
Set playback sample rate in Hz. Default 48000.
.TP
.B "\-c channels, \-\-channels channels"
Select one or two playback channels. Default two.
.TP
.B "\-b frames, \-\-blocksize frames"
Set PortAudio callback block size in sample frames. Default 960.
.TP
.B "\-g dB, \-\-gain dB"
Set playback gain in dB. Default zero.
.TP
.B "\-p milliseconds, \-\-playout milliseconds"
Set initial playout delay in milliseconds. Default 100.
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-notch"
Enable tone notching for sessions.
.TP
.B "\-s, \-\-voting"
Enable automatic session selection; also disables automatic stereo
positioning.
.TP
.B "\-S, \-\-autosort"
Automatically sort the session display.
.TP
.B "\-q, \-\-quiet"
Disable the curses display.
.TP
.B "\-u milliseconds, \-\-update milliseconds"
Set curses display update timeout in integer milliseconds. Default 100.
.TP
.B "\-f pathname, \-\-config pathname"
Read INI configuration first, then apply command-line overrides. Sections
include [audio], [display], [radio], and [repeater].
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Enable diagnostics.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH INTERACTION
Use up/down to select sessions. m/u mute/unmute the selected session; M/U act
on all sessions. r resets the selected playout buffer and R resets all
buffers. Plus/minus changes volume; left/right changes stereo position.
Uppercase Q exits; lowercase q toggles quiet display. Consult the installed
monitor help for the full key list.
.SH NOTES
A session may not appear until data arrives, particularly when receiver
squelch suppresses output. Missing metadata can prevent interpretation of
dynamically assigned payload formats. --center is present in the option table
but is not implemented by the command-line handler; use center in [audio]
configuration instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
List playback devices and listen to an output group:
.PP
.nf
monitor \-L
monitor hf\-pcm.local
monitor \-q \-I hf\-pcm.local \-I vhf\-pcm.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
radiod(8), control(1), pcmrecord(1)
.PP
Project documentation: https://github.com/ka9q/ka9q-radio
